From a412be84855c6859a12f21f9b71a6dad82076bad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 05:20:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Guided Story: stop feeding the model things it cannot use
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Job #69001 rendered the mother as two visibly different women across three
scenes and returned one character in a shot that asks for three. Two files
were responsible.

videoGen/guidedScenePrompt.ts — three faults in 60 lines:

  1. `identity reference /objects/4/uploads/<uuid>` went into the prompt as
     text. That is tenant storage — no video model resolves it — so the
     identity direction did nothing while reading as though it worked, and a
     wrong-person render looked like a model quality problem. Identity travels
     as an attached reference; generateVideo already takes `assetIds` and
     providers/atlascloud.ts already turns them into `reference_images` with
     @ImageN naming. This file just has to stop competing with it.
  2. The backdrop direction carried the whole storyboard — "Scene 1: …
     Scene 2: … Scene 3: …", the last truncated mid-sentence at "She...." —
     into every scene, handing the model three shots and asking for one.
  3. The cast was described but never closed, so a scene asking to end on all
     three together could return one person and be, on its own terms, correct.

Role descriptions were personality ("anxious but witty"), which is not
visible, so the model invented a look per scene — appearance now comes from
the reference and the prompt carries only the wardrobe, which is actionable.
A character with no approved outfit used to put the literal word "MISSING"
into the prompt. The logo path became a request for logo space, since the
path was never openable and the bare word invites the model to invent one.

`referenceLabels` is the hook for the reference work: pass "@Image1",
"@Image2" in cast order once assetIds really are attached.

lib/characters.ts — the reference sheet is not only reviewed by a person, it
is the asset registered with Atlas and handed to the model as the character's
identity. The prompt asked for "a clean, useful labeled grid" and "small
useful detail and color swatches", and got exactly that: panel headers, hex
chips, a "Detail Swatch" caption, garbled labels ("FRONT BOUP: FACE & HAIR",
"Full-Body Side (Sick)"), and a title naming a character who is not the one
in the story. A reference model reads all of it as part of the subject. The
prompt now bans every non-photographic element and asks the face close-up to
fill its panel, since face pixels are the identity signal and a five-panel
grid is already stingy with them.

That grid is still a compromise. ByteDance's guidance is a full-body frontal
and a face close-up as two separate single-subject assets in one group;
reaching that is a change to the approval flow and to registration, not to
this prompt.

Existing sheets keep their old look until each character's sheet is
regenerated and re-approved.

The two replaced tests asserted the broken behaviour verbatim — one pinned
the prompt wording "preserves the existing visual prompt wording and order",
the other required the "labeled grid" that caused this.

